---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What counts as medium heat chilli?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Medium heat chilli is 10,000–20,000 Scoville Heat Units (SHU) — noticeably warm without the intense burn of high-heat varieties. Representative Chinese varieties are Xian pepper and Jinta. It is the largest-volume band because it suits the broadest range of products without overwhelming other flavours."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is medium heat chilli used for?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Medium heat chilli is the workhorse for general seasoning, chilli paste, pizza and snack topping, and everyday spice blends where heat should be present but not dominant. Its role is warmth and colour, not a headline burn — which is why it is the default band for mass-market products."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which varieties should I specify for 10,000–20,000 SHU?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Xian pepper and Jinta are the main 10,000–20,000 SHU varieties; Erjingtiao sits at 15,000–30,000 SHU. Jinta suits flakes and powder for bright colour; Erjingtiao adds aroma and is used for chilli paste. Heat and aroma vary independently, so name the variety and SHU window in your specification."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How does medium heat compare to sweet paprika?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Medium heat (10,000–20,000 SHU) is roughly 20–40× hotter than sweet paprika (under 500 SHU). That gap is why paprika is bought for colour and medium chilli for warmth — they are not interchangeable in a recipe."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Medium Heat Chilli (10,000–20,000 SHU) | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Medium heat chilli (10,000–20,000 SHU) — Xian pepper and Jinta for seasoning, chilli paste and pizza topping. China manufacturer, ISO 9001 & HACCP."
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/dried-chillies/">Dried Chillies</a> &rsaquo; Medium Heat
    </nav>
    <h1>Medium Heat Chilli (10,000–20,000 SHU)</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Noticeably warm, not overwhelming — the 10,000–20,000 SHU band is the workhorse of everyday
      chilli seasoning, made from varieties like Xian pepper and Jinta.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Medium heat is the 10,000–20,000 SHU band — warm enough to be noticed, mild enough for everyday seasoning, snacks and general use. It is the largest-volume band in chilli because it suits the broadest range of products without overwhelming other flavours.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What counts as medium heat chilli?</h2>
    <p>
      Medium heat sits between low-heat (under 10,000 SHU) and high-heat (20,000–50,000 SHU). It
      delivers clear warmth and aroma, but stays mild enough for products aimed at a broad audience —
      the reason it is the default choice for pizza, snack seasoning and general spice blends.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Variety</th><th>Heat (SHU)</th><th>Region</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Xian pepper</strong></td><td>10,000 – 20,000</td><td>Inner Mongolia, Xinjiang</td><td>Medium powder, flakes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Jinta</strong></td><td>10,000 – 20,000</td><td>Gansu</td><td>Flakes, powder (glossy red)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Erjingtiao</strong></td><td>15,000 – 30,000</td><td>Sichuan, Guizhou</td><td>Chilli paste, aroma</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is medium heat chilli used for?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pizza and pasta chilli flakes</li>
      <li>Everyday seasoning blends and spice mixes</li>
      <li>Chilli paste and table sauces</li>
      <li>Snack and crisp seasoning</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify medium heat chilli</h2>
    <p>
      Name the variety and the SHU window, then the product form. For flakes choose Jinta or Xian;
      for an aromatic paste choose Erjingtiao. Confirm moisture (under 13%) and, for powder, mesh
      (60–80 mesh standard).
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat values are representative per variety. Confirm your exact SHU window, form, mesh and
      moisture when you inquire.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Medium heat in numbers: warmth without the burn</h2>
    <p>
      Medium heat (10,000–20,000 SHU) is the most economical heat band to work in, and the numbers
      explain why. It sits roughly <strong>20–40× hotter than sweet paprika</strong> (under 500 SHU)
      and <strong>2.5–5× milder than extra-hot</strong> Jixin (50,000–70,000 SHU). That wide margin
      is forgiving: a 10% over-dose of medium chilli shifts a blend by only a few thousand SHU,
      whereas the same over-dose of extra-hot shifts it by over 5,000 SHU.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Variety</th><th>SHU</th><th>Relative to sweet paprika</th><th>Relative to extra-hot</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Xian pepper</td><td>10,000 – 20,000</td><td>20–40× hotter</td><td>3.5–7× milder</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jinta</td><td>10,000 – 20,000</td><td>20–40× hotter</td><td>3.5–7× milder</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Erjingtiao</td><td>15,000 – 30,000</td><td>30–60× hotter</td><td>2.3–4.7× milder</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="data-note">
      Ratios use band midpoints as arithmetic estimates. Heat is non-linear, so use them for
      direction, not exact substitution.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What counts as medium heat chilli?</h3>
      <p>Medium heat chilli is 10,000–20,000 Scoville Heat Units (SHU) — noticeably warm without the intense burn of high-heat varieties. Representative Chinese varieties are Xian pepper and Jinta.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is medium heat chilli used for?</h3>
      <p>Medium heat chilli is the workhorse for general seasoning, chilli paste, pizza and snack topping, and everyday spice blends where heat should be present but not dominant.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which varieties should I specify for 10,000–20,000 SHU?</h3>
      <p>Xian pepper and Jinta are the main 10,000–20,000 SHU varieties; Erjingtiao sits at 15,000–30,000 SHU. Jinta suits flakes and powder; Erjingtiao adds aroma and is used for chilli paste. Name the variety and SHU window in your specification.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How does medium heat compare to sweet paprika?</h3>
      <p>Medium heat (10,000–20,000 SHU) is roughly 20–40× hotter than sweet paprika (under 500 SHU). That gap is why paprika is bought for colour and medium chilli for warmth — they are not interchangeable in a recipe.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Request Medium Heat Quotation</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/heat-levels/high-heat/" style="margin-left:8px;">High Heat →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
